# Original HomeCostEngine manuscript. Project dollar amounts are hypothetical USD.
# Reference supplies only the broad book sequence, not wording or examples.
CHAPTERS = []
def chapter(title, deck, paragraphs, example, rows, conclusion, prompts, checks, task):
    CHAPTERS.append(dict(title=title,deck=deck,paragraphs=paragraphs,example=example,rows=rows,conclusion=conclusion,prompts=prompts,checks=checks,task=task))

chapter('Getting started with your project', 'A useful budget begins with a decision, not a price.', [
'Write the problem the renovation must solve in one sentence. “Make the kitchen usable for two cooks” is a decision brief; “buy new cabinets” is already a solution. A clear problem gives you room to choose a less disruptive solution when the first proposal exceeds your ceiling.',
'Define the rooms, the finish level and the conditions you will retain. Record who lives in the home, whether work can happen during the day, and what must remain available. A bathroom project in a one-bath home has a different temporary-use problem from the same work in an empty property.',
'Choose three non-negotiable outcomes and rank everything else. Distinguish a target budget from an absolute spending limit. The difference is not permission to spend; it is protection against pretending that a comfortable target and available funds are the same thing.',
'At this stage, mark uncertain facts as unknown. Do not convert a guess about wiring, framing or moisture into an included repair. Give each unknown an owner and a date for verification. The first useful deliverable is a brief that another person can read without you explaining it.'
], 'A homeowner has $30,000 available but wants the complete project to stay within $27,000. The working plan must include the project reserve inside $27,000; the extra $3,000 is not counted twice as contingency.', [('Available funds','$30,000'),('Working project ceiling','$27,000'),('Funds kept outside this project','$3,000')],
'Agree on the working ceiling before shopping. If the scope cannot fit, change the scope or timing explicitly rather than silently moving the ceiling.',
['Problem this renovation must solve','Three required outcomes, in order','Included rooms and retained items','Working ceiling / absolute limit / funding source','Facts to verify, responsible person and deadline'],
['Everyone approving purchases has read the brief.','A decision date exists for every open selection.','Temporary living needs are included in the scope.'], 'Send the same one-page brief to every contractor invited to quote.')

chapter('Renovation cost foundations', 'Separate the quantity, the rate and the scope behind a total.', [
'A total price hides at least three decisions: how much work is required, what is included in that work, and the rate used to price it. Keep those decisions visible. An installed rate may already contain materials, labor, equipment and contractor overhead; adding those costs again produces a misleading budget.',
'Label every line with a unit. Square feet of flooring, linear feet of trim, individual fixtures and hours of investigation are different quantities. A room floor area is not its wall area, and a building footprint is not necessarily roof surface area. Match the measurement to the task before using a calculator.',
'Use current written local evidence for rates. Note its date, location, scope, tax treatment and expiry. An online planning range can help you identify questions, but it cannot confirm access, substrate condition, local labor availability or the work a contractor will accept.',
'Keep quantity uncertainty separate from rate uncertainty. If you need 300 square feet of finished flooring, the purchase quantity may be larger. Show the chosen waste factor as an assumption, then ask the installer whether the layout and product require a different allowance.'
], 'For an illustrative flooring purchase, measured area is 300 sq ft and the selected ordering allowance is 8%. Material costs $4 per sq ft, while installation is quoted on the measured area at $3 per sq ft. These are invented teaching rates, not market estimates.', [('Purchase quantity: 300 × 1.08','324 sq ft'),('Material: 324 × $4','$1,296'),('Installation: 300 × $3','$900'),('Subtotal before other scope','$2,196')],
'This subtotal excludes tax, delivery, demolition, disposal, trim and preparation. Add only items not already included in the quoted rates.',
['Task and exact unit being priced','Measured quantity / source / date','Ordering allowance and reason','Rate evidence and what it includes','Excluded work requiring a separate quote'],
['Installation and purchase quantities are distinguished.','Each rate has a dated source.','Included labor or materials have not been added twice.'], 'Build five scope lines with explicit units before entering any prices.')

chapter('Understanding your budget structure', 'A reserve is part of the total funding requirement.', [
'Use five buckets: defined base work, unresolved selection allowances, separately approved upgrades, owner-side costs and a reserve for uncertainty. The categories prevent an attractive base bid from disguising the amount you must actually fund. An allowance is a placeholder for a known selection; a reserve covers uncertainty. They are not interchangeable.',
'Owner-side costs can include storage, temporary accommodation, delivery paid directly, design work and permit fees if excluded from the contractor price. Assign responsibility for every line. A permit paid by the contractor belongs inside that proposal, not in a second owner line.',
'Calculate the reserve against a clearly named basis. “Ten percent contingency” is incomplete until you say ten percent of what. The teaching percentage used here is an assumption, not a recommendation for every project. Hidden conditions and incomplete design may require a different risk response.',
'The useful number is the all-in plan compared with the available ceiling. If it does not fit, remove or defer a defined item. Reducing the reserve solely to make the spreadsheet balance does not make the underlying risk disappear.'
], 'A hypothetical plan has $18,000 of defined work, a $2,000 fixture allowance and $1,000 of owner costs. A planning reserve of 10% is applied only to the $20,000 work-plus-allowance basis.', [('Defined work','$18,000'),('Selection allowance','$2,000'),('Owner costs','$1,000'),('Reserve: 10% × $20,000','$2,000'),('All-in funding plan','$23,000')],
'If the ceiling is $22,000, the plan is $1,000 over before work starts. Resolve that gap through a documented scope or funding decision.',
['Base work total and included lines','Selection allowances with exact boundaries','Owner-side costs and payment responsibility','Reserve basis / percentage / amount / risks covered','All-in plan minus working ceiling'],
['Every cost appears in one bucket only.','The reserve basis can be reproduced.','An over-budget result has a real decision attached.'], 'Create one summary sheet and keep all supporting quotes behind it.')

chapter('Write a scope that can be priced', 'Describe the finished result and the work needed to get there.', [
'A scope is more than a shopping list. Describe existing conditions, removal, preparation, installation, protection and restoration. “Install vanity” can omit disconnecting plumbing, removing the old unit, repairing the wall, reconnecting fixtures and disposing of debris. The missing verbs are often the missing costs.',
'Use item codes so a scope line survives revisions. For each code, record location, quantity, product or performance requirement, inclusions, exclusions and the acceptance condition. Add photos or drawings with dates. Do not use an unverified owner measurement as an installation authorization.',
'Where the condition is unknown, ask for an investigation or a conditional unit rate. For example, damaged roof decking can be priced per approved sheet or square foot with a stated thickness and installation scope. The repair still needs field confirmation; a budget allowance is not a diagnosis.',
'Assign interfaces between trades. Who reconnects a dishwasher after flooring? Who patches the wall after electrical work? A sentence identifying responsibility is more useful than a vague promise that a project is turnkey. Confirm scope changes in writing before accepting a revised total.'
], 'A hypothetical vanity line is split into five scope codes. The numbers below are merely an example of how a complete scope is organized, not an estimate for your bathroom.', [('B01 / remove and dispose','$150'),('B02 / owner-selected vanity allowance','$700'),('B03 / installation labor','$250'),('B04 / plumbing reconnection','$200'),('B05 / wall patch and finish','$100'),('Defined line total','$1,400')],
'A $950 proposal covering only the unit and installation cannot yet be compared with this $1,400 complete line. Clarify the three missing tasks first.',
['Scope code / room / photo or drawing reference','Existing condition and retained items','Remove / prepare / install / restore / dispose','Product specification and measurable completion condition','Trade interfaces / exclusions / unknown conditions'],
['Removal and restoration are addressed.','Unknown conditions have a verification step.','Each interface has one named responsible party.'], 'Rewrite the vaguest line in your current proposal using five action verbs.')

chapter('Measure quantities before choosing rates', 'Record the calculation so someone else can check it.', [
'Start with a sketch and label each dimension. Record feet and inches consistently; do not type 10 feet 6 inches as 10.6 feet. Ten feet six inches is 10.5 feet. Keep the raw measurements beside the converted decimal values so transcription errors can be found.',
'Break irregular spaces into simple shapes, calculate each, then add them. For a floor, distinguish the area being finished from openings and exclusions. For walls, calculate perimeter times height and record which openings are deducted. The installer may price small openings differently because cutting and masking still take time.',
'Purchase quantities depend on product packaging and layout. Round up to full cartons or containers only after applying the selected ordering allowance. Never treat this allowance as a structural design rule or assume unused material can be returned.',
'For concrete, volume in cubic yards equals length in feet times width in feet times thickness in inches divided by 324. Thickened edges require their additional volume separately. A calculator can check arithmetic; it cannot determine structural thickness or reinforcement.'
], 'An illustrative rectangular slab measures 12 ft × 10 ft × 4 in. The owner is checking quantity only; the dimensions are not a construction design. A chosen 8% ordering allowance is applied after calculating geometric volume.', [('Geometric volume: 12 × 10 × 4 ÷ 324','1.4815 cu yd'),('With 8% allowance: 1.4815 × 1.08','1.60 cu yd'),('Separate thickened-edge volume','Not included')],
'Confirm the supplier minimum, truck access, placement method and final order increment. Do not add another blanket allowance without explaining what it covers.',
['Sketch ID / room / measurement date','Raw dimensions and units','Shape-by-shape quantity calculation','Allowance / package size / rounded purchase quantity','Excluded areas or additional volumes to confirm'],
['Inches have been converted correctly.','The quantity matches the rate unit.','Structural specifications come from the responsible professional.'], 'Check the arithmetic with homecostengine.com/calculators/concrete and retain the sketch.')

chapter('Research and normalize contractor quotes', 'Compare the same job before comparing the bottom line.', [
'Give bidders the same dated scope and ask them to identify substitutions, allowances and exclusions. A lower number can represent a narrower job. Build a comparison with one column per bidder and one row per scope line, then mark included, excluded, allowance or unclear.',
'Use a separate comparison adjustment for missing work. Obtain evidence for that adjustment instead of inventing a precise number. An adjusted comparison is your analysis; it is not a revised offer from the contractor. Record which adjustment still needs confirmation.',
'Check identity, licensing where required, insurance, references and contract terms. The FTC advises checking contractors and obtaining written estimates and a written contract before work. Verify requirements through the relevant local authority rather than assuming a directory listing proves authorization. [1]',
'Price is only one decision dimension. Record schedule, proposed products, supervision, cleanup, payment milestones, warranty and the route for resolving a dispute. Do not turn these into an invented star rating. Explain why a meaningful difference matters to your project.'
], 'Bid A is $18,500 but excludes disposal and permit administration. Written scope-matched quotes for those omissions are $900 and $600. Bid B is $19,800 and includes both. All other relevant scope is assumed equal for this teaching example.', [('Bid A as received','$18,500'),('Add disposal / permit administration','$1,500'),('Bid A adjusted comparison','$20,000'),('Bid B comparable total','$19,800'),('Difference','$200')],
'The apparent $1,300 advantage for A reverses after documented exclusions. Confirm assumptions with both bidders before selection.',
['Bidder / quote date / expiry / scope version','Base price and allowance boundaries','Excluded tasks with evidence for each adjustment','Comparable total and unresolved assumptions','Schedule / credentials / warranty / decision rationale'],
['No unclear item is silently marked included.','Adjustments have a source and date.','The selected contract identifies payment and change rules.'], 'Use homecostengine.com/calculators/contractor-quote-comparison for a second arithmetic check.')

chapter('Review the budget in deliberate passes', 'Replace uncertainty with evidence without losing earlier decisions.', [
'Build the budget in passes: a rough scope, a measured scope, written quotes, a selected contract and live cost tracking. Label the version and date. A number that was useful for deciding whether to investigate may be too weak for authorizing a purchase.',
'Maintain separate columns for approved budget, committed cost, paid cost and forecast remaining cost. Committed means an obligation you have accepted; paid is the cash already transferred. Adding paid amounts to the full committed contract double-counts the same expense.',
'At each review, update the forecast for the complete project. The forecast equals known paid costs plus unpaid obligations plus expected uncommitted work. Compare that figure with the funding ceiling and record why it changed. A late invoice is not the only sign of an overrun.',
'Choose a decision cadence that matches the work. Before a selection deadline, update that allowance. Before concealed work closes, resolve inspection and documentation questions. Keep a short decision log: choice, approver, budget effect, schedule effect and evidence. This protects the project from forgetting why a tradeoff was made.'
], 'A $20,000 signed contract has $6,000 paid and $14,000 unpaid. Expected owner purchases are $2,000 and the remaining reserve is $1,500. The full forecast is $23,500, not $29,500.', [('Paid costs','$6,000'),('Unpaid committed obligations','$14,000'),('Expected uncommitted purchases','$2,000'),('Remaining reserve','$1,500'),('Forecast including reserve','$23,500')],
'The $6,000 is already part of the $20,000 contract. Record it for cash tracking, but never add it on top of the full contract again.',
['Budget version / date / next review','Paid to date with invoice references','Unpaid commitments and payment dates','Uncommitted work and remaining reserve','Forecast / ceiling / variance / decision required'],
['Paid and committed amounts reconcile.','Cancelled obligations are removed with evidence.','The forecast includes work not yet contracted.'], 'Run this review before approving any upgrade, not only at month end.')

chapter('Kitchen remodeling without allowance surprises', 'Make the cabinet, countertop and trade interfaces explicit.', [
'A kitchen budget should identify whether the layout stays or moves. Keeping a sink in the same location is a different scope from relocating drainage, supply and electrical connections. Separate cabinetry, counters, appliances, flooring, lighting, ventilation and temporary kitchen needs rather than using one unexplained room total.',
'For cabinets, count doors and drawers but also record cabinet boxes, dimensions, fillers, panels, hardware and finish. Refacing, door replacement and full cabinet replacement are different services. For countertops, identify template, cutouts, edge treatment, backsplash, removal and reconnection responsibility.',
'An allowance needs a boundary: product only or installed? Does it include tax, shipping and hardware? Record the selected product and the expected adjustment before ordering. The same applies when appliances are owner-supplied; delivery and installation may sit in different contracts.',
'Check dependencies before choosing finishes. An appliance specification can change cabinet openings or electrical requirements. Have the responsible trades confirm the selections before fabrication. The cost of correcting an incompatible opening can exceed an apparent saving on the product itself.'
], 'A hypothetical cabinet hardware allowance covers 28 handles at $8 each. The chosen handle is $12, and installation is already included in the base contract. Tax and delivery are excluded here to keep the comparison clear.', [('Original allowance: 28 × $8','$224'),('Chosen hardware: 28 × $12','$336'),('Selection increase','$112'),('Extra installation charge','$0 included')],
'Only the $112 material difference belongs in this change. Confirm tax and freight separately and do not pay installation twice.',
['Layout retained or changed / trade confirmation','Cabinet box / door / drawer / hardware schedule','Countertop scope and appliance specifications','Allowance inclusions / selected prices / differences','Temporary kitchen and critical delivery dates'],
['Every appliance fits its approved opening.','Disconnection, reconnection and disposal are assigned.','Selections are approved before fabrication.'], 'Use the kitchen cost guide to prepare questions, then replace planning numbers with local quotes.')

chapter('Bathroom scope beyond the visible fixtures', 'Budget the assembly and access, not just the finish.', [
'The visible tile and fixtures are only part of a bathroom project. Record demolition, substrate preparation, the specified waterproofing assembly, plumbing connections, ventilation, electrical work, finish restoration and disposal. Ask who verifies concealed conditions and who documents the completed work before it is covered.',
'Choose products as a compatible assembly with the installer; this workbook does not specify a waterproofing design. A tile price alone cannot establish whether a shower proposal includes drains, corners, penetrations, preparation or testing appropriate to the selected system.',
'Distinguish a cosmetic refresh from opening walls or moving fixtures. If there is evidence of moisture damage, obtain an assessment of the cause before pricing the surface repair. EPA guidance emphasizes addressing moisture problems rather than treating visible mold as only a finish issue. [3]',
'For an occupied home, plan bathroom access and water interruptions. Record who protects adjacent rooms and what happens if the inspection or a delayed fixture changes the schedule. Those decisions can affect storage and temporary accommodation even if the contractor base bid stays unchanged.'
], 'A hypothetical tile area is 90 sq ft. A selected 12% ordering allowance gives 100.8 sq ft. Tile is sold in cartons covering 10 sq ft. The order is rounded up to 11 cartons, not rounded down to 10.', [('Net area','90 sq ft'),('With allowance: 90 × 1.12','100.8 sq ft'),('Cartons: round up 100.8 ÷ 10','11'),('Purchased coverage','110 sq ft')],
'This calculation covers tile quantity only. It does not include waterproofing, substrate, setting materials, trim or installation.',
['Fixture locations retained / moved / approval needed','Assembly specification and installer responsibilities','Measured tile areas / layout allowance / carton size','Moisture investigation and concealed-condition process','Access plan / interruptions / restoration / schedule'],
['The waterproofing scope is written.','The reason for moisture damage is addressed.','Installation materials are not confused with tile cost.'], 'Ask for the assembly scope before comparing two shower prices.')

chapter('Roof replacement and hidden decking', 'Separate measured roof surface from conditional repairs.', [
'A roof budget starts with surface area and the complete assembly, not the house floor area. Ask for the measured roof area, pitch and access assumptions, number of tear-off layers, material specification and disposal scope. A roofing square means 100 square feet; keep that unit visible.',
'Identify underlayment, flashing, edge details, ventilation, penetrations and protection of the property. Do not assume every item is included because the quote says roof replacement. Ask which components are reused, which are replaced and what evidence supports that choice.',
'Decking repairs may remain conditional until tear-off exposes the surface. Request an agreed repair unit, material specification, installation scope, photo record and approval process. A per-sheet rate is hard to compare with a per-square-foot rate until the sheet coverage and included work match.',
'Keep the decision about repair necessity with the qualified contractor and any responsible designer. The homeowner budget should show how approved additional work is measured and charged, including any minimum charge. Avoid writing an unlimited blank allowance for everything discovered after removal.'
], 'For a teaching example, a roof measures 2,000 sq ft. An additional 160 sq ft of decking repair is approved after photos. The agreed installed rate is $4 per sq ft. A 4 × 8 ft sheet covers 32 sq ft geometrically; cutting and layout may change actual sheets required.', [('Roof area: 2,000 ÷ 100','20 squares'),('Approved decking repair','160 sq ft'),('Repair: 160 × $4','$640'),('Geometric sheet equivalents: 160 ÷ 32','5')],
'The five sheet equivalents are a unit conversion, not a guaranteed purchasing count. Follow the contracted measurement and charging method.',
['Roof area / measurement source / pitch / access','Tear-off layers / disposal / property protection','Assembly inclusions and reused components','Decking unit / specification / photo / approval rule','Conditional repair cap and review contact'],
['The quote uses roof surface area.','Decking repair rates include a defined scope.','Additional work requires evidence and approval.'], 'Keep the replacement estimate separate from the conditional decking record.')

chapter('Flooring and the surface underneath', 'A material bargain can hide preparation and transition work.', [
'Map flooring by room, material and installation method. Record demolition, disposal, moisture or flatness assessment, preparation, underlayment where specified, installation, transitions, baseboards and door adjustments. A price for laying flooring may include none of the work that makes it possible.',
'Subfloor repair is not the same task as surface preparation. Ask how each is assessed and charged, especially after water damage. The proposed product manufacturer and installer should determine acceptable substrate conditions. This book does not prescribe a universal flatness or moisture limit.',
'Confirm which rooms can be used during the work. Moving furniture, storing materials, acclimation where required and protecting finished floors can affect sequence. A staged installation may have different labor assumptions from an empty-house job.',
'For purchase quantity, show measured area, exclusions, ordering allowance and carton rounding. Keep unopened spare material separately identified. A reserve carton intentionally retained for future repairs is not installation waste and should not vanish inside a percentage nobody can explain.'
], 'A hypothetical project has 420 sq ft of finished floor. An 8% ordering allowance yields 453.6 sq ft. Cartons cover 22 sq ft. The owner orders 21 cartons, giving 462 sq ft, before any separate spare carton decision.', [('Net finished area','420 sq ft'),('With 8%: 420 × 1.08','453.6 sq ft'),('Cartons: round up 453.6 ÷ 22','21'),('Coverage: 21 × 22','462 sq ft')],
'Price installation on the quantity specified in the proposal, which may differ from purchased coverage. Record preparation as its own defined scope.',
['Room schedule and measured finished areas','Existing finish / removal / disposal responsibility','Substrate assessment and conditional preparation rate','Carton coverage / allowance / retained spare stock','Transitions / trim / doors / furniture / sequence'],
['Preparation assumptions are written.','The selected product suits the approved substrate.','The order rounds up to complete packages.'], 'Ask the installer to verify the takeoff before placing a nonreturnable order.')

chapter('Painting quantities and preparation levels', 'Specify the surface condition and coat system before comparing gallons.', [
'Paint quantity begins with the area actually being coated. For walls, multiply room perimeter by wall height, then document openings. Ceilings, trim and doors use separate measurements or counting rules. A floor area shortcut cannot explain the paintable surface of rooms with different heights.',
'Record the surface condition, cleaning, repairs, sanding where appropriate, primer requirement, finish coats and protection. A repaint over sound existing finish is a different job from repairing extensive damaged surfaces. Agree on what a completed surface should look like and how color changes or additional coats are handled.',
'Use coverage from the selected product data and ask whether texture, application method or porosity changes the assumption. Round purchases by actual container size. Do not assume a universal coverage rate or count primer and finish paint as the same material.',
'In older homes, disturbing existing paint can introduce lead-related requirements. Ask qualified contractors about applicable EPA renovation rules for pre-1978 housing before surface preparation. This budget exercise does not authorize unsafe sanding or removal. [2]'
], 'An illustrative room is 12 × 10 ft with 8 ft walls. Deducted openings total 40 sq ft. Two finish coats are planned. The example product coverage is assumed to be 350 sq ft per gallon per coat; check the actual product label.', [('Wall area: 2 × (12 + 10) × 8','352 sq ft'),('Net area: 352 − 40','312 sq ft'),('Two-coat coverage: 312 × 2','624 sq ft'),('Finish paint: 624 ÷ 350','1.783 gal'),('Rounded whole-gallon purchase','2 gal')],
'The two gallons exclude primer, ceilings, trim and any extra coats. Preparation labor must be priced independently if not included.',
['Wall / ceiling / trim / door quantities','Surface condition and agreed preparation scope','Primer and finish product / coverage / coats','Openings deducted / container sizes / purchase rounding','Protection / access / cleanup / older-paint questions'],
['Coverage is per coat.','Primer is a separate product line.','Older coatings are assessed before disturbance.'], 'Cross-check wall quantity with homecostengine.com/calculators/paint.')

chapter('HVAC replacement as a complete system', 'Compare installation scope alongside equipment.', [
'List the existing heating and cooling equipment, observed comfort issues and parts expected to remain. An equipment model and nominal capacity do not describe the full installation. Record ducts, controls, electrical supply, condensate handling, removal, commissioning and any building work.',
'ENERGY STAR advises appropriate sizing based on the home rather than assuming a larger unit is better. Ask the contractor to explain the sizing assessment and document the proposed match of equipment and retained distribution. [4] This workbook is not a load calculation or a basis for selecting capacity.',
'If comparing a heat pump with another replacement, hold the comfort scope and retained ducts constant where appropriate. Identify any changes in electrical work, backup heat, controls or duct repairs. Do not compare one complete proposal with another that postpones necessary supporting work.',
'Assess operating costs with explicit assumptions for energy use, tariffs and maintenance. Savings are uncertain until the baseline and proposed system assumptions are supported. Keep rebates and tax benefits outside the guaranteed funding plan until eligibility, timing and actual availability are confirmed.'
], 'In a hypothetical scope comparison, Proposal A is $9,000 plus $1,200 of excluded duct repairs and $600 of electrical work. Proposal B is $10,500 including the same supporting work. Neither number is a market price.', [('A equipment and installation','$9,000'),('A supporting work','$1,800'),('A comparable total','$10,800'),('B comparable total','$10,500'),('Difference','$300')],
'Confirm that commissioning, controls and warranties also match. A $300 difference alone does not establish which system better serves the home.',
['Existing equipment / retained ducts / comfort problems','Sizing assessment and proposed matched equipment','Electrical / controls / duct / condensate inclusions','Commissioning evidence / warranty / service access','Comparable total / operating assumptions / incentives'],
['Capacity is supported by a home-specific assessment.','Retained ducts have been considered.','Unconfirmed incentives do not fund required payments.'], 'Request a scope-matched proposal before comparing equipment brands.')

chapter('Windows and doors by opening', 'Count the installation tasks, not only the units.', [
'Build a schedule with one code per opening. Record location, approximate dimensions, operation, product series, glazing, hardware and finish. Ask the installer to confirm final dimensions. Replacement within an existing opening and reconstruction of that opening are different scopes.',
'Clarify removal, disposal, flashing, air sealing, interior trim, exterior restoration and painting. A low unit price can omit most of those tasks. Confirm access assumptions for upper floors and how concealed deterioration is assessed and approved.',
'Compare product performance using the actual configuration. NFRC labels provide performance ratings that support comparisons; U-factor concerns heat transfer and SHGC concerns solar heat admitted. [5] Choose with local climate, orientation and professional guidance rather than assuming the lowest value in every category is always the right goal.',
'Before ordering, record who owns measurement errors, substitutions and delivery damage. A budget worksheet cannot resolve contract terms after a nonreturnable order has been placed. Keep the approved schedule and the supplier confirmation together.'
], 'For six identical hypothetical windows, quoted installed base cost is $700 each. Disposal is $40 per opening and interior repainting is a separate $300 line. The base installed rate excludes those two items.', [('Six installed units: 6 × $700','$4,200'),('Disposal: 6 × $40','$240'),('Interior repainting','$300'),('Comparable scope total','$4,740')],
'If another proposal includes disposal and repainting, compare it with $4,740, not $4,200. Structural opening changes remain excluded here.',
['Opening schedule / location / installer measurements','Product configuration and performance-label reference','Removal / flashing / sealing / trim / paint / disposal','Concealed-damage unit and approval method','Order approval / delivery / damage / warranty terms'],
['Final dimensions are installer-confirmed.','The same glazing and opening method are compared.','Restoration work has a named responsible party.'], 'Photograph each opening and tie it to the schedule code.')

chapter('Plumbing and water-related work', 'Define the connection and restoration boundary.', [
'Plumbing budgets often fail at the boundary between a fixture and the building. A fixture purchase can exclude valves, adapters, connection labor, access openings, drain changes and restoration. Record those separately and ask the plumber to confirm compatibility with existing services.',
'For water heater work, identify the selected system and the scope of removal, venting where relevant, drainage, electrical or fuel connections, access and permit responsibilities. Replacement with similar equipment is not automatically identical to the old installation requirements.',
'For a suspected leak, separate investigation from the eventual repair. Ask what the diagnostic charge includes, how findings are recorded and who authorizes additional work. A repair allowance is not evidence that the leak source has been located.',
'Water shutoffs and access matter in occupied homes. Agree on the expected interruption and communication process. Keep inspection records, product documents and photos of concealed work for closeout. Use qualified professionals for installation and local requirements; this chapter teaches quote organization, not plumbing procedures.'
], 'A hypothetical replacement quote separates a $1,200 unit, $450 connection labor, $150 removal and $200 of confirmed supporting work. Wall restoration remains a separately quoted $300 owner cost.', [('Equipment','$1,200'),('Connection labor','$450'),('Removal and supporting work','$350'),('Plumbing scope subtotal','$2,000'),('Separate wall restoration','$300'),('Combined funding need','$2,300')],
'The $300 restoration should not be added if a revised plumbing contract already includes it. Track the scope version.',
['Fixture / system / exact model or performance scope','Existing connection conditions to verify','Diagnostic findings and authorization limit','Removal / connections / venting / permit / restoration','Shutdown plan / inspection / documents / warranty'],
['Equipment compatibility has been checked.','Restoration is explicitly included or separately priced.','Diagnostic work has a defined authorization boundary.'], 'Ask who repairs every opening created to reach the plumbing.')

chapter('Electrical upgrades with defined endpoints', 'A device count is not a circuit or service design.', [
'Start with what the project must power and where the equipment will be located. Record the appliance or device specifications and ask a qualified electrician to assess the existing installation. A list of outlets cannot determine service capacity, circuit design or protective requirements.',
'Separate devices, wiring routes, panel or service work, utility coordination, inspection and finish repairs. If walls will already be open, coordinate the timing, but do not assume that open access makes electrical work free. Document which trade patches and paints after the electrician finishes.',
'Obtain a written scope for any panel replacement. Ask what remains, what changes, how interruption is managed, and which responsibilities sit with the utility or permitting authority. Do not treat the panel label alone as proof that an upgrade is required.',
'Budget for decisions before equipment purchases. A cooking appliance, HVAC unit or charging system can change supporting electrical scope. Have the professional confirm the specified loads and installation needs before placing an order. This is a planning checklist, never a guide to perform electrical work.'
], 'In an illustrative room quote, eight devices are priced at $85 each within a specifically defined installation scope. The electrician also lists a $400 supporting-work item. Paint restoration is quoted separately at $220.', [('Device scope: 8 × $85','$680'),('Defined supporting electrical work','$400'),('Electrical subtotal','$1,080'),('Separate paint restoration','$220'),('Combined funding need','$1,300')],
'The per-device figure is not transferable to a different wiring route or code scope. Keep the written assumptions attached.',
['Equipment specifications and required locations','Electrician assessment / retained installation','Devices / wiring / panel / utility scope boundaries','Permit / inspection / interruption / restoration owner','Approved total and unresolved dependencies'],
['Design decisions come from the qualified professional.','Supporting work is not hidden in a device count.','Appliance selections and electrical scope agree.'], 'Freeze the equipment schedule before asking for the final electrical price.')

chapter('Smart upgrades and energy decisions', 'Compare useful outcomes with ongoing obligations.', [
'A smart renovation is one whose decisions remain understandable after installation. Technology may help, but only when it solves a defined problem. Describe the desired outcome, such as scheduling lighting or improving access control, before choosing a particular connected product.',
'Record purchase cost, installation, any hub, subscriptions, replacement consumables and the expected supported life. Ask what works if the internet fails, a subscription stops or a household member cannot use the app. Include account handover and documentation in the closeout scope.',
'For energy upgrades, compare the complete installed scope and supported operating assumptions. Do not label a product as paying for itself without showing the incremental cost and an evidence-based savings estimate. Comfort and maintenance may still matter when the financial result is uncertain.',
'A simple payback calculation divides incremental upfront cost by annual savings. It ignores financing, price changes, maintenance differences and the value of money over time. Use it as an initial comparison, then test less favorable assumptions rather than presenting a single year as a guarantee.'
], 'A hypothetical optional upgrade costs $900 more initially. Assumed gross savings are $180 per year but it adds a $60 annual subscription. Net assumed savings are $120; simple payback is 7.5 years. Savings have not been measured.', [('Incremental upfront cost','$900'),('Assumed annual gross savings','$180'),('Annual subscription','$60'),('Assumed net savings','$120'),('Simple payback: $900 ÷ $120','7.5 years')],
'If gross savings fall to $60, net savings are zero and there is no finite simple payback under these assumptions. Do not divide by zero.',
['Problem to solve / manual alternative','Installed cost / hub / subscription / ongoing costs','Offline operation / support / account ownership','Savings evidence / conservative case / payback limits','Acceptance test and household handover'],
['Recurring costs are included.','A less favorable scenario has been tested.','Essential functions have a usable fallback.'], 'Approve technology for a clear household benefit, not a projected resale promise.')

chapter('Change orders and project cash flow', 'Protect the reserve and the payment calendar separately.', [
'A change order should describe the reason, exact work, price adjustment, schedule effect and approval. Keep a change log even for credits. A verbal upgrade can become a disputed invoice if nobody records whether the price included associated labor or restoration.',
'Distinguish a selection overage from an unforeseen repair. The first consumes the upgrade or selection budget; the second may use the project reserve after verification. Moving both silently into contingency makes it impossible to tell whether risk or preference is driving the overrun.',
'Build a cash calendar from the signed payment terms, expected owner purchases and confirmed delivery dates. A project can be within its total budget and still face a cash shortfall in a particular week. Do not depend on an unconfirmed rebate arriving before a required payment.',
'Link payment records to invoices and the agreed milestones. Confirm work and contractual obligations before approving payment, and seek local professional advice where legal rights, deposits or lien documents are involved. This book does not set a universally lawful deposit percentage or payment schedule.'
], 'A hypothetical project starts with a $2,000 reserve. A documented hidden-condition repair uses $700. A separate optional finish upgrade costs $400 and is funded from the upgrade budget, not charged to the reserve.', [('Starting project reserve','$2,000'),('Approved hidden-condition repair','−$700'),('Remaining reserve','$1,300'),('Optional upgrade charged elsewhere','$400')],
'If next week requires a $5,000 milestone payment plus $1,200 in owner purchases, plan $6,200 of available cash regardless of the remaining reserve figure.',
['Change ID / reason / scope / evidence','Cost increase or credit / tax / related work','Funding bucket and reserve movement','Schedule effect / approval / revised contract total','Next payment dates / amounts / cash available'],
['Changes are agreed before work proceeds.','Optional upgrades do not silently consume the reserve.','Upcoming cash requirements have been checked.'], 'Reconcile the signed change log with the latest forecast before paying the next invoice.')

chapter('Closeout, records and future maintenance', 'Finish the paperwork as carefully as the visible work.', [
'Create a closeout list before the final walkthrough. It should cover the contracted finish, operation, unresolved defects, cleanup, documentation and any required inspection or permit closure. Tie each open item to a location, responsible person and agreed completion date.',
'Collect product models, serial numbers, finishes, manuals, warranty terms, service contacts and photos of concealed work where available. Store them by room or system, not only in a contractor email thread. These records help a future repair professional understand what was installed.',
'Keep accepted work, open items and disputed items distinct. A walkthrough note is not a substitute for the contract or local legal advice about payment. Record what both parties agree will happen next and retain copies of invoices, approvals and proof of payment.',
'Once the project closes, separate unused project funds from a future maintenance reserve. Maintenance savings are not evidence that the renovation came in below its agreed scope. Compare final cost with the original budget and classify differences as scope changes, selection changes, hidden conditions or estimate errors. That explanation makes the next project easier to plan.'
], 'A hypothetical completed project has $20,000 of contracted work, $1,100 of signed changes and $900 in owner purchases. Final cost is $22,000 against an all-in ceiling of $23,000. No obligations remain unpaid in this example.', [('Contracted work','$20,000'),('Signed net changes','$1,100'),('Owner purchases','$900'),('Final project cost','$22,000'),('Ceiling less final cost','$1,000')],
'The $1,000 is genuinely unspent only after all obligations are reconciled. Moving it to maintenance is a new allocation, not an extra renovation cost.',
['Walkthrough items / location / owner / due date','Required inspection and closeout documents','Models / warranties / manuals / service contacts','Final invoices / payments / outstanding obligations','Final cost / original ceiling / lessons / next review'],
['No open obligation is hidden by an unspent balance.','The household has operating instructions and records.','Final variance is explained by category.'], 'Save a dated closeout folder and schedule the first maintenance review.')

